<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coosawattee River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Coosawattee River is a river in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which joins the Conasauga near Calhoun to form the Oostanaula. In the 14th to 16th centuries its valley was home to Mississippian towns, among them the Little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> site near Carters, which archaeologists widely identify as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> of Coosa visited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a> in 1540. Carters Dam now impounds part of the river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coosawattee-river.jpg" alt="Coosawattee River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomson200 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coosawattee_River,_Ellijay.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Coosawattee is 49.3 miles (79.3 km) long. It begins at the confluence of the Ellijay and Cartecay rivers in the city of Ellijay, in central Gilmer County, and flows west through the foothills of the North Georgia mountains. Its tributaries include Mountaintown Creek, Talking Rock Creek and Salacoa Creek. Below Carters Dam it forms the boundary between Murray and Gordon counties before entering Gordon County, and near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-echota">New Echota</a> it meets the Conasauga River to form the Oostanaula, itself a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coosa-river">Coosa River</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The country around the confluence was the centre of the Cherokee Nation's territory in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>. In the early 1820s, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> had forced them to cede their lands in eastern Tennessee and they had moved south, the Cherokee made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-echota">New Echota</a>, near the meeting of the rivers, their capital. It remained so until their removal in 1838. The American writer James Dickey based the fictional Cahulawassee River of his first novel, Deliverance (1970), on the Coosawattee, and the book was made into a feature film directed by John Boorman in 1972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In Murray County the river is impounded by Carters Dam, completed in 1977, the tallest earthen dam east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a>; the lake behind it, Carters Lake, lies mostly in Gilmer County, and below the dam the river flows into a reregulation reservoir. The Coosawattee Foundation, based in Calhoun and established in 1987, works to protect Native American resources in the American Southeast through excavation, education and advocacy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Coosawattee River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Coosa formed in the 14th and 15th centuries among Mississippian towns along the <b>Coosawattee River</b> in north-west Georgia, with its capital probably at the Little Egypt site near modern Carters.</blockquote><a href="/state/coosa-chiefdom">Coosa chiefdom</a></li></ul></section>
